WEBVTT

1
00:00:00.000 --> 00:00:03.780
Theorem 3.4.11. If f is differentiable at x-naught, y-naught,

2
00:00:03.780 --> 00:00:07.835
the tangent plane to its graph S exists, is unique,

3
00:00:07.835 --> 00:00:10.500
and has this equation. Here's why.

4
00:00:10.900 --> 00:00:13.950
First, slice S with the plane y equals y-naught.

5
00:00:13.950 --> 00:00:19.063
The curve z equals f of x, y-naught has slope f sub x at x-naught,

6
00:00:19.063 --> 00:00:23.825
so its tangent line, r one, has direction vector one, zero, f sub x.

7
00:00:24.225 --> 00:00:28.935
Similarly, slicing with x equals x-naught gives a tangent line, r two,

8
00:00:28.935 --> 00:00:32.075
with direction vector zero, one, f sub y.

9
00:00:32.475 --> 00:00:35.330
Any tangent plane must contain both lines.

10
00:00:35.330 --> 00:00:39.779
So we want a normal vector n equals a, b, c,

11
00:00:39.779 --> 00:00:43.025
perpendicular to both direction vectors: a homogeneous system.

12
00:00:43.425 --> 00:00:47.255
Its augmented matrix is already in reduced row echelon form,

13
00:00:47.255 --> 00:00:49.585
with a and b basic, and c free.

14
00:00:49.585 --> 00:00:52.800
Setting c equal to one gives the basic solution:

15
00:00:52.800 --> 00:00:57.225
n equals negative f sub x, negative f sub y, one.

16
00:00:57.625 --> 00:01:01.920
So the tangent plane is n dot x minus x-naught, y minus y-naught,

17
00:01:01.920 --> 00:01:05.140
z minus f of x-naught, y-naught, equals zero.

18
00:01:05.140 --> 00:01:07.775
Solving for z gives the theorem's equation:

19
00:01:07.775 --> 00:01:10.375
the graph of the linear approximation L.
